  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Battery Groups · Last reviewed 2026-09-22</p>
      <h1>Group 31 Battery: Dimensions, Capacity, CCA and Why Stud Terminals Matter</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Group 31 is the heavy-duty battery that shows up everywhere — trucks, buses, boats, RVs, off-grid
        solar banks and medical carts. It is a <strong>physical size standard</strong> (about
        <strong>330 × 173 × 240 mm</strong>), and most Group 31 batteries cluster around
        <strong>~100 Ah capacity and ~900 CCA under SAE J537</strong>, with <strong>stud terminals</strong> instead of the
        automotive post.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Group 31 is a <a href="/lead-acid/battery-group-sizes/">BCI group size</a> — a 12V case of
        roughly 330 × 173 × 240 mm that typically carries ~100 Ah and ~900 CCA (SAE J537). It exists in three
        duties: <strong>starting</strong> (high CCA), <strong>deep-cycle</strong> (thick plates, repeated discharge) and
        <strong>dual-purpose</strong> (a compromise of both). The defining detail is the <strong>stud terminal</strong>, which
        handles the higher currents and ring-lug connections of heavy-duty installations.
      </div>

      <h2>What is a Group 31 battery?</h2>
      <p>
        Group 31 is one of the large-format groups defined by the Battery Council International (BCI). The
        group number describes the <strong>case dimensions and terminal layout</strong>, not the performance — two Group
        31 batteries from different makers fit the same tray but can differ widely in capacity, CCA and
        construction. The <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/group31/">Group 31 reference page</a> in
        our data section lists the cross-verified typical specification: 330 × 173 × 240 mm, ~100 Ah, ~900 CCA
        (SAE J537, −18°C), stud or stud/post combination terminals.
      </p>
      <p>
        The format grew out of North American heavy-duty trucking, where it became the standard electrical
        workhorse. Its blend of size and output has since spread it to marine, RV, off-grid and industrial
        duty — which is why the same group number appears on batteries with very different internal builds.
      </p>

      <h2>Group 31 at a glance</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Typical Group 31 value</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Voltage</strong></td><td>12V (6 cells)</td><td>Nominal lead-acid voltage</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Dimensions</strong></td><td>~330 × 173 × 240 mm</td><td>Confirm height &amp; terminal style per brand</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Capacity</strong></td><td>~100 Ah (20-hour rate)</td><td>Varies ~90–110 Ah by maker and duty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>CCA</strong></td><td>~900 A</td><td>SAE J537 (−18°C); state the standard when comparing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Terminals</strong></td><td>Stud, or stud + post</td><td>3/8″-16 stud common; SAE automotive post on some</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Values are cross-verified typical ranges from the World Battery Hub data section, not a substitute for a
        specific battery's datasheet. CCA is standard-dependent — a figure without its test standard is not comparable
        (see <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">what CCA is</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>Why stud terminals define this group</h2>
      <p>
        The Group 31 terminal is the group's most practical feature. Where passenger cars use tapered
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-terminal-types/">SAE posts</a>, heavy-duty installations bolt <strong>ring lugs</strong> onto a
        threaded <strong>stud</strong> — usually 3/8″-16 — with a nut and washer. A bolted connection survives vibration,
        resists loosening, and carries the continuous currents of winches, inverters and hydraulic pumps that a
        spring-clamped post connection handles poorly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catch is the <strong>layout variants</strong>: stud-only, stud-plus-post, and different stud positions. Before
        ordering, check which terminal pattern your cables and accessories expect — the
        <a href="/lead-acid/dual-purpose-battery/">dual-purpose</a> and marine variants are the ones that most often add the
        automotive post for clamp-on accessories.
      </p>

      <h2>Starting, deep-cycle or dual-purpose?</h2>
      <p>
        Group 31 is a <em>case</em>, and the same case holds three very different batteries:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Starting Group 31</strong> — many thin plates for maximum plate surface area, high CCA for cranking large
          diesels. Built to give short, hard bursts, not deep discharges. Compare
          <a href="/lead-acid/deep-cycle-vs-starting/">deep-cycle vs starting construction</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deep-cycle Group 31</strong> — fewer, thicker plates that tolerate repeated discharge to 50% and recharging,
          at the cost of some cranking punch. The choice for house banks, trolling motors and solar storage.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Dual-purpose Group 31</strong> — plate design between the two, good enough to crank and able to cycle
          modestly. Convenient where one battery must do both, with neither done as well as a purpose-built unit.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The nameplate alone will not tell you the duty. The datasheet will: a deep-cycle unit quotes cycle life and
        reserve capacity rather than a headline CCA figure, and its CCA is usually lower than a starting unit's. Read
        the <a href="/procurement/how-to-read-battery-datasheet/">datasheet, not the sticker</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>AGM vs flooded Group 31</h2>
      <p>
        Group 31 exists in both flooded and <a href="/lead-acid/agm-battery/">AGM</a> construction. AGM versions dominate in
        marine, RV and off-grid duty because they are sealed, position-tolerant, vibration-resistant and charge
        faster; flooded versions remain common in heavy trucks where cost per Ah matters and maintenance is routine.
        The trade-offs mirror the general <a href="/lead-acid/agm-vs-efb-vs-flooded/">AGM vs EFB vs flooded</a> comparison — with
        one Group 31-specific note: the AGM version of the same group is often slightly shorter than the flooded one,
        so check height against the hold-down.
      </p>

      <h2>Where Group 31 is used</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Heavy trucks and buses</strong> — often as the main cranking battery or one of a bank; see the <a href="/lead-acid/truck-battery/">truck battery guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Marine</strong> — house banks, trolling motors and dual-purpose duty; see the <a href="/lead-acid/marine-battery/">marine battery guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>RV and camper</strong> — house battery for lighting, pumps and inverters; see the <a href="/lead-acid/rv-battery/">RV battery guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Off-grid solar</strong> — small battery banks where the 100 Ah format is a building block; see the <a href="/lead-acid/solar-battery/">solar battery guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Industrial and mobility</strong> — lift gates, pallet movers, scrubbers, medical equipment.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Group 31 is a <strong>size standard</strong> (~330 × 173 × 240 mm), not a performance spec — verify Ah, CCA and duty per maker.</li>
          <li>Typical values are <strong>~100 Ah and ~900 CCA (SAE J537)</strong>; CCA must always carry its test standard.</li>
          <li><strong>Stud terminals</strong> are the group's signature — check stud-only vs stud+post before ordering.</li>
          <li>Choose the <strong>duty</strong> deliberately: starting, deep-cycle or dual-purpose are different batteries in the same case.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the CCA figure stated with its test standard (SAE J537 vs EN vs JIS), and does the quoted Ah carry a discharge rate?</li>
        <li>Which terminal layout is specified — stud-only or stud+post — and does it match the fleet's cable lugs?</li>
        <li>Is the duty stated (starting / deep-cycle / dual-purpose), and does the plate construction support it?</li>
        <li>Does the supplier's drawing match the tray, including height and hold-down, for both flooded and AGM versions?</li>
        <li>For bank purchases, are matched units (same maker, lot and age) specified, since series or parallel banks degrade unevenly otherwise?</li>
        <li>Does the quotation separate the battery spec from accessories, and is a <a href="/procurement/battery-quotation-comparison/">like-for-like comparison</a> possible across suppliers?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is a Group 31 battery?</h3>
      <p>A BCI-standardized 12V battery group measuring about 330 × 173 × 240 mm, typically rated around 100 Ah and 900 CCA under SAE J537, with stud or stud/post terminals. It is the most common heavy-duty format across trucks, marine, RV and off-grid use.</p>
      <h3>What are the dimensions of a Group 31 battery?</h3>
      <p>Approximately 330 mm long × 173 mm wide × 240 mm high (about 13 × 6.8 × 9.4 inches). Height and terminal style vary between brands and between flooded, AGM and lithium drop-in versions, so always confirm against the tray and the manufacturer drawing.</p>
      <h3>Is a Group 31 battery deep-cycle or starting?</h3>
      <p>Both variants exist. Starting versions maximize CCA with many thin plates; deep-cycle versions use thicker plates for repeated discharge; dual-purpose versions compromise between the two. Check the duty in the datasheet before buying.</p>
      <h3>What is the difference between Group 31 and Group 27?</h3>
      <p>Group 31 is larger — about 330 mm long versus about 306 mm for Group 27 — and typically carries roughly 100 Ah and 900 CCA versus roughly 90–95 Ah and 800 CCA. They are not interchangeable unless the tray and cables accept the bigger case.</p>
      <h3>Can I replace a Group 31 with a lithium battery of the same group?</h3>
      <p>Lithium drop-in batteries are sold in Group 31 case format, but the charging system, temperature limits and battery management requirements differ from lead-acid. The vehicle or system must be verified for lithium compatibility, including the alternator and charge profile.</p>

